// Advent of Code 2025, Day 1 — Secret Entrance
//
// A dial numbered 0..99, starting at 50. Each rotation is `L<n>` or `R<n>`.
//   part one: how many rotations LEAVE the dial on 0
//   part two: how many CLICKS land on 0, during a rotation or at the end
//
// THIS PUZZLE HITS BOTH OF THE LANGUAGE'S KNOWN GAPS AT ONCE, which is why it
// is worth keeping next to the code rather than only in a write-up.
//
//   1. MODULO. There is no `/`, `%`, `div` or `rem` in the lexer — ticket 38,
//      raised from 2019 Day 1. A dial IS modular arithmetic, so this puzzle is
//      unwritable without it. It comes through the FFI, and the wrapper below
//      is the one every language needs because `rem` truncates: Erlang's
//      `-18 rem 100` is `-18`, not `82`.
//
//   2. PARSING. `L68` is a letter and a number, and the language has no
//      `string`, no `binary` and no way to take either apart. The shell turns
//      each line into a SIGNED integer before the compiler ever sees it —
//      L68 becomes -68, R43 becomes 43 — which means a real part of the puzzle
//      is being solved outside the language. Stated plainly rather than hidden:
//      see ../../README.md.
//
// What IS the language's own work: the modular wrap, the click-by-click
// simulation, the two folds, and the fact that every function below is
// exhaustive with no catch-all.

module Day01

using :erlang {
    int rem(int a, int b)
}

// The dial has 100 positions, so every arithmetic result comes back through
// here. Two `rem`s because the first can be negative: `rem` truncates toward
// zero, so `-18 rem 100` is `-18` and the `+ 100` is what makes it 82.
private int Wrap(int n)
Wrap(n) -> :erlang.rem(:erlang.rem(n, 100) + 100, 100)

// A dial reading is 0 or it is not, and that is the whole of the question the
// puzzle asks of it. Two clauses, exhaustive over `int`.
private int Hit(int pos)
Hit(0) -> 1
Hit(n) -> 0

// --- part one: only where a rotation ENDS -----------------------------------
//
// The delta is already signed, so a whole rotation is one wrap. No simulation
// is needed to answer part one, and doing it this way keeps the two parts
// honestly different rather than one being the other with a flag.
private int Ends(list<int> rotations, int pos, int zeros)
Ends([], pos, zeros)          -> zeros
Ends([d, ..rest], pos, zeros) ->
    var next = Wrap(pos + d)
    Ends(rest, next, zeros + Hit(next))

public int PartOne(list<int> rotations)
PartOne(rs) -> Ends(rs, 50, 0)

// --- part two: every CLICK ---------------------------------------------------
//
// "if the dial were pointing at 50, a single rotation like R1000 would cause
// the dial to point at 0 ten times" — so the count is not a property of where
// the rotation lands, and the dial has to be turned one click at a time.
//
// `Step` is the direction, `left` the clicks remaining. It returns the position
// AND the running count, which is what a tuple is for.
private (int, int) Spin(int pos, int step, int left, int zeros)
Spin(pos, step, 0, zeros)              -> (pos, zeros)
Spin(pos, step, left, zeros) when left > 0 ->
    var next = Wrap(pos + step)
    Spin(next, step, left - 1, zeros + Hit(next))
Spin(pos, step, left, zeros) when left < 0 -> (pos, zeros)

// A signed delta splits into a direction and a click count. `Sign` and `Size`
// are separate so neither needs a division.
private int Sign(int d)
Sign(0) -> 1
Sign(d) when d > 0 -> 1
Sign(d) when d < 0 -> -1

private int Size(int d)
Size(d) when d >= 0 -> d
Size(d) when d < 0  -> -d

private int Clicks(list<int> rotations, int pos, int zeros)
Clicks([], pos, zeros)          -> zeros
Clicks([d, ..rest], pos, zeros) ->
    var (next, hits) = Spin(pos, Sign(d), Size(d), zeros)
    Clicks(rest, next, hits)

public int PartTwo(list<int> rotations)
PartTwo(rs) -> Clicks(rs, 50, 0)
